Gr4Ig — Guiding Principles
Version 1.1 — 2026-03-08
Status: Stable

1. The Computer Serves the Human, Not the Reverse
The experience adapts to the human, not the other way around. Every architectural decision should be evaluated against this: does it reduce the cognitive load on the user, or does it transfer complexity to them? Complexity that cannot be eliminated must be absorbed by the system, not delegated upward.

2. Flow Is the Organizing Principle
Work is not a collection of discrete tasks. It is a continuous, branching flow of thought and action. The system is organized around enabling and sustaining that flow — predicting where it goes next, removing obstacles before they appear, and supporting forks and returns without losing context. The window-and-application paradigm is explicitly rejected as a relic of task-centric computing.

3. The System Grows to Know the User
Gr4Ig develops an increasingly accurate understanding of the user over time — their patterns, their priorities, their working style, and the shape of their obligations. This knowledge is never declared by the user and never frozen. It is continuously observed, inferred, and revised. The depth of this relationship is what makes genuine adaptation possible.

4. Context Is Everything
The system maintains continuous awareness of the user's context — what they are doing, what they were doing, what is coming, and what is competing for their attention. Surfacing the right information at the right moment, including information the user did not know they needed, is a core capability, not a feature. Context is the primary input to every system decision.

5. Data Belongs to the User and Flows Freely
Information is never siloed by the tool that created it. All data lives in a shared, semantically rich store that is owned entirely by the user and accessible across every service. Portability is not a migration feature — it is the baseline. No component may treat data as its own.

6. Intelligence Is Infrastructure
The cognitive layer is not an assistant sitting above the system. It is woven into the foundation — driving the experience, informing the flow engine, deepening the user relationship, and operating continuously in the background. AI is not a feature of Gr4Ig. It is the nervous system.

7. Predict, Prepare, Present
The system does not wait to be asked. It reasons ahead of the user's current position in their flow, prepares what will likely be needed next, and presents it at the moment it becomes relevant. Reactive assistance is a fallback, not the design target.

8. Voice Is a First-Class Interaction
Speech is not an alternative input method bolted onto a keyboard-centric system. Voice interaction is designed in from the foundation — ambient, interruptible, and capable of operating in parallel with other activity. The keyboard is one interaction mode among several, not the default.

9. Open at Every Layer
Gr4Ig is free and open source from the kernel up. No component may introduce a dependency that compromises the user's full ownership of their environment — no data harvesting, no subscription gates, no corporate incentives embedded in the stack. Longevity and community are the goals. The architecture must be legible and extensible to contributors who arrive after the foundation is laid.

10. Simple Components, Emergent Capability
The system is built on the Unix philosophy — small, sharp components with clean interfaces. Complexity emerges from composition, not from monolithic design. Any component should be replaceable without requiring the system to be redesigned. The architecture is the asset, not any specific implementation.

This document is a living record. Update as understanding develops.
